schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Source Systems: Knowledge That Outlives Turnover"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    Defines a "source system" as a living knowledge environment (wiki, repo,
    forum, garden…) rather than a one-off link — one that has a steward,
    keeps getting new material added, and offers a return path to credit and
    trace claims back to their origin. Contrasts this with a static "link
    list" that rots. Ties provenance (recorded origin/history) to the
    principle "inclusion does not mean endorsement," gives real named tools
    (Obsidian, Logseq, TiddlyWiki, CITATION.cff, ADR/MADR) that already encode
    these properties, and frames stewardship of a source system as a
    governance problem (Ostrom's commons-design principles), not just tidying.
  audience: small-team leads maintaining wikis/repos/knowledge bases who want them to survive contributor turnover
  known_tensions:
    - "add-broadly-classify-lightly (low friction intake) vs. mark-maturity-honestly (don't let raw material pass as endorsed)"
    - "a link list is easy to start and cheap to maintain; a source system requires an actual steward and return path, which is more work up front"
  related_concepts:
    - source-system
    - provenance
    - digital-garden
  related_resources:
    - Obsidian
    - Logseq
    - TiddlyWiki
    - "Citation File Format (CITATION.cff)"
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/source-systems.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

